<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
  <title>Final Exam Review</title>

<section>
<title>Successful students will be able to...</title>

<ul>
  <li>
    <p>
      develop a DE to model a physical situation (e.g.
      Newton’s law of cooling, population growth, radioactive decay, Torricelli’s law, newton’s second law applied to spring mass systems, mixture problems using tanks)
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (1.2) determine if conditions are sufficient to provide for the existence and uniqueness of solutions to the IVP
      <me>
        \frac{dy}{dx}= f(x,y), \ \ y(x_{0})=y_{0}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (1.2) generate and interpret slope fields associated with DEs and utilize them to estimate solutions to the IVP <m>\frac{dy}{dx}= f(x,y), \ y(x_{0})=y_{0}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (1.3) implement the method of separation of variables.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (1.4) implement the method of solving linear, first-order DEs by using an integrating factor.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (1.5) determine the stability of equilibrium solutions to autonomous DEs <m>\frac{dy}{dx}= f(y)</m>.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (1.6) apply Euler’s method to estimate solutions to first-order DEs.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (5.1) convert a higher order DE to a first-order system of DEs.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (2.1, 2.5) apply the superposition principle to define general solutions.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      use a Wronskian to determine whether or not a set of solutions to a DE are linearly independent.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (2.3) solve <m>n^{th}</m> order, homoegeneous, constant coefficient, linear DEs.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (2.5) implement the method of undetermined coefficients to find a particular solution to certain <m>n^{th}</m> order linear constant coefficient, nonhomogeneous DEs.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (3.1) state the definition of the Laplace transform.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (3.1) compute a Laplace transform from the definition.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (3.1) compute an inverse Laplace transform using a table.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (3.2, 3.3, 3.4) solve IVPs (particularly those that involved discontinuous functions) using a Laplace transform.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (4.3) represent a system of linear equations using a matrix.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (4.2) perform matrix arithmetic (addition, multiplication, transpose, etc.).
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (4.3) utilize elementary row operations to perform gaussian elimination (putting a matrix in row echelon form and reduced row echelon form).
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (4.3) solve linear systems of equations through gaussian elimination.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (4.2) determine (using various criteria) when the inverse of a square matrix <m>A</m> exists and compute it when it does.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (4.6) compute the determinant of a matrix using both the permutation definition and cofactor expansion.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (4.6) understand how elementary row operations affect the determinant of a matrix.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (4.4) identify if a given set <m>S</m> is a vector space under a particular method of addition and scalar multiplication.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (4.4) identify if a subset <m>S</m> of a given vector space is a subspace.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (4.4) write the null space (kernel) of a matrix as the span of a set of vectors (by using gaussian elimination).
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (4.4) identify if a given set of vectors (in <m>\mathbb{R}^{n}</m>) are linearly dependent or linearly independent.
      [Writing one as a linear combination of the others if dependent.]
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (4.4) identify a basis for a finite-dimensional vector space (e.g.
      for the solution space of a given DE, for the null space of a matrix <m>A</m>).
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (5.4) state the definition of an eigenvalue and an eigenvector.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (5.4, 5.7) characterize an eigenspace associated with eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> as the null space of <m>A-\lambda I</m> and write it as the span of a set of vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^{n}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (5.4) compute (real and complex) eigenvalues and eigenvectors.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (5.8) determine if a given square matrix <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.
      If it is, compute a matrix <m>P</m> such that <m>A=PDP^{-1}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (5.8) compute a matrix expoenential <m>e^{A}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      determine whether a set of <m>n</m>-vector valued functions are linearly independent or linearly dependent using a Wronskian.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (5.3) apply the superposition principle to systems of DEs in the form <m>{\textbf{x}}' = A(t){\textbf{x}}+{\textbf{f}}(t)</m>.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (5.5) solve homogeneous systems of DEs of the form <m>{\textbf{x}}' = A{\textbf{x}}</m> and determine the stability of the equilibrium point.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      (5.4, 5.5) determine real-valued solutions to homogeneous systems of DEs of the form <m>{\textbf{x}}' = A{\textbf{x}}</m> in which <m>A</m> has complex eigenvalues.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-finalexamreview">
    <title>Sample Questions</title>

  <!-- 1 -->
  <exercise label="tf-invertible-matrix">
    <title>Invertibility of a Matrix</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The matrix 
        <m>
          A = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix}
        </m>
        is invertible.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>True.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. The determinant is 
            <m>\det(A) = -1 \neq 0</m>,  
            so the matrix is invertible.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement><p>False.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No. The matrix has nonzero determinant, so it is invertible.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>

  </exercise>

  <!-- 2 -->
  <exercise label="tf-difference-eigenvectors">
    <title>Difference of Eigenvectors</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The difference of two eigenvectors 
        <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> of a matrix 
        <m>A</m> is always an eigenvector of <m>A</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p>True.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No. Only scalar multiples of a single eigenvector are guaranteed to remain eigenvectors.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>False.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. The difference of eigenvectors corresponding to the 
            <em>same</em> eigenvalue is an eigenvector, but this fails for
            eigenvectors from different eigenvalues.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>

  </exercise>

  <!-- 3 -->
  <exercise label="tf-diagonalizable-square">
    <title>Diagonalizability of a Matrix and its Square</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> is diagonalizable,
        then <m>A^2</m> is diagonalizable.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>True.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. If <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>, then  
            <m>A^2 = PD^2P^{-1}</m>, which is also diagonalizable.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement><p>False.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No. The diagonalizability of <m>A</m> guarantees the same for <m>A^2</m>.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>

  </exercise>

  <!-- 4 -->
  <exercise label="tf-geom-alg-multiplicity">
    <title>Geometric vs. Algebraic Multiplicity</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In an <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, the geometric 
        multiplicity of each eigenvalue is less than or equal to its algebraic multiplicity.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>True.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. This is a fundamental theorem of eigenstructure:  
            <m>1 \le \text{geom mult} \le \text{alg mult}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement><p>False.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No. Geometric multiplicity can never exceed algebraic multiplicity.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>

  </exercise>

  <!-- 5 -->
  <exercise label="tf-solution-count-20x10">
    <title>Solutions to a Linear System</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For a <m>20 \times 10</m> matrix <m>A</m>,
        the equation <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> has either zero or infinitely many solutions.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p>True.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No. Such a system can have exactly one solution if consistent and full column rank.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>False.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. A tall matrix <m>A</m> (more equations than unknowns)
            can have zero, one, or infinitely many solutions.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>

  </exercise>

  <!-- 6 -->
  <exercise label="tf-diagonalizable-real-complex">
    <title>Diagonalizability over <m>\mathbb{R}</m> vs. <m>\mathbb{C}</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        There exist matrices which are diagonalizable over the complex numbers
        but not over the real numbers.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>True.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. For example, a rotation matrix in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> 
            has complex eigenvalues but no real eigenvalues.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement><p>False.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No. Complex diagonalizability does not guarantee real diagonalizability.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>

  </exercise>

  <!-- 7 -->
  <exercise label="tf-de-bounded-solutions">
    <title>Boundedness of Differential Equation Solutions</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For the differential equation 
        <m>x''(t) + x(t) = \sin t</m>, all solutions are bounded.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p>True.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No. The forcing term <m>\sin t</m> resonates with the homogeneous solution.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>False.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. There exist unbounded solutions due to resonance:
            the particular solution contains a term linear in <m>t</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>

  </exercise>

  <!-- 8 -->
  <exercise label="tf-similarity-transpose">
    <title>Similarity and Transposes</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If a <m>2\times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> is similar to 
        a matrix <m>B</m>, then the transpose <m>A^T</m> is similar to <m>B^T</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>True.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. If <m>A = PBP^{-1}</m>, then  
            <m>A^T = (P^{-1})^T B^T P^T</m>, showing similarity.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement><p>False.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No. Transposition preserves similarity.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>

  </exercise>

  <!-- 9 -->
  <exercise label="tf-eigenvalues-invertible">
    <title>Eigenvalues and Invertibility</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <m>3\times 3</m> matrix <m>A</m> is invertible 
        if and only if all its eigenvalues are positive.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p>True.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No. Invertibility requires eigenvalues to be nonzero, not necessarily positive.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>False.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct. A matrix with eigenvalues <m>-2, 3, -1</m> is invertible but not all positive.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>

  </exercise>

  <!-- 10 -->
  <exercise label="tf-subspace-inequalities">
    <title>Subspace Defined by Inequalities</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The set of vectors 
        <m>\begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{pmatrix}</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>
        satisfying <m>x_1 \ge 2x_2</m> and <m>\frac12 x_1 \le x_2</m>
        is a subspace.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>True.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. The two inequalities together force 
            <m>x_1 = 2x_2</m>, describing a line through the origin — a subspace.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement><p>False.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No. The set actually forms a one-dimensional subspace.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>

  </exercise>

<exercise label="similar-matrices-eigenvalues">
  <title>Similarity and Eigenvalues</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose
      <m>
        A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{pmatrix},\quad
        B = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix},\quad
        C = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{pmatrix},\quad
        D = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix},\quad
        E = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
      Which matrices have the same eigenvalues and are similar?
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Similar matrices must have exactly the same eigenvalues, including multiplicities.  
      Compute the eigenvalues of each matrix and compare them.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>B</m>, <m>A</m>, and <m>E</m> have the same eigenvalues and are similar to each other.  
      Also, <m>C</m> and <m>D</m> have the same eigenvalues and are similar.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      We compute eigenvalues one matrix at a time:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>A</m> has eigenvalues <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>B</m> has eigenvalues <m>-1</m> and <m>1</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>E</m> also has eigenvalues <m>-1</m> and <m>1</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>C</m> has eigenvalues <m>2</m> and <m>0</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>D</m> has eigenvalues <m>2</m> and <m>0</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Matrices that are similar must have exactly the same eigenvalues with the same multiplicities.  
      So:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>E</m> share the same two eigenvalues <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m>.  
          Therefore, they are similar to one another.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>C</m> and <m>D</m> both have eigenvalues <m>2</m> and <m>0</m>.  
          Thus, they are similar to each other as well.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise label="match-phase-portraits">
  <title>Matching Linear Systems to Phase Portraits</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Match each linear system of differential equations below with its corresponding
      phase portrait shown in <xref ref="phase-portraits-ABCD"/>.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <m>
          \mathbf{x}' =
          \begin{pmatrix}
            0 \amp -1 \\
            1 \amp 0
          \end{pmatrix}
          \mathbf{x}
        </m>
      </li>

      <li>
        <m>
          \mathbf{x}' =
          \begin{pmatrix}
            0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1
          \end{pmatrix}
          \mathbf{x}
        </m>
      </li>

      <li>
        <m>
          \mathbf{x}' =
          \begin{pmatrix}
            2 \amp 2 \\
            0 \amp -2
          \end{pmatrix}
          \mathbf{x}
        </m>
      </li>

      <li>
        <m>
          \mathbf{x}' =
          \begin{pmatrix}
            2 \amp 2 \\
            0 \amp 2
          \end{pmatrix}
          \mathbf{x}
        </m>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <!-- Example of how you will include the figure in PreTeXt -->
    
    <figure xml:id="phase-portraits-ABCD">
      <caption>Phase portraits (I)–(IV).</caption>
      <image source="\images\PhasePortraitABCD.jpg" width="90%">
      <description>A horizontal row of four phase portraits labeled (I), (II), (III), and (IV).
Portrait (I) shows an improper unstable node: trajectories move outward, bending to the right, with a dominant expanding direction and a secondary curved direction.
Portrait (II) shows a center: closed circular trajectories rotating around the origin.
Portrait (III) shows a saddle: trajectories approach along one axis and diverge along another, with characteristic hyperbolic curves.
Portrait (IV) shows vertical straight-line trajectories: all motion is upward or downward along parallel vertical lines, with no horizontal component. </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Compute the eigenvalues for each system.  
      Look for features such as rotation (imaginary eigenvalues), 
      vertical lines (a zero eigenvalue with a positive eigenvalue), 
      unstable nodes (repeated positive eigenvalues), and saddle behavior.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      (a) matches (II),  
      (b) matches (IV),  
      (c) matches (I),  
      (d) matches (III).
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      We analyze each system by computing eigenvalues and identifying qualitative behavior.
    </p>

    <ul>

      <li>
        <p>
          System (a):
          <m>
            \begin{pmatrix}
              0 \amp -1 \\
              1 \amp 0
            \end{pmatrix}
          </m>
          has eigenvalues <m>\pm i</m>, indicating pure rotation.  
          This corresponds to portrait (II), the center with circular motion.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          System (b):
          <m>
            \begin{pmatrix}
              0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
          </m>
          has eigenvalues <m>0</m> and <m>1</m>.  
          Motion is purely vertical with exponential growth in the second component.  
          This matches portrait (IV).
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          System (c):
          <m>
            \begin{pmatrix}
              2 \amp 2 \\
              0 \amp -2
            \end{pmatrix}
          </m>
          has eigenvalues <m>2</m> and <m>-2</m>, a classic saddle structure.  
          This matches portrait (I), which shows the unstable–stable saddle dynamics.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          System (d):
          <m>
            \begin{pmatrix}
              2 \amp 2 \\
              0 \amp 2
            \end{pmatrix}
          </m>
          has repeated eigenvalue <m>2</m> with only one eigenvector, producing an
          improper (degenerate) unstable node.  
          This matches portrait (III).
        </p>
      </li>

    </ul>

  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="linear-system-3x6-solution">
  <title>Solving a Linear System and Describing Its Solution Set</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the linear system
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      \begin{aligned}
      x_1 + 2x_2 + 3x_3 + 3x_4 + 2x_5 + x_6 \amp = 3,\\
      \quad x_2 + 2x_3 + 2x_4 + x_5 \amp = 2,\\
      \quad x_3 + x_4 \amp = 1.
      \end{aligned}
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>Answer the following:</p>

    <ol>
      <li>Rewrite the system in matrix form <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m>.</li>
      <li>Row reduce the augmented matrix <m>[A \mid \mathbf{b}]</m>.</li>
      <li>Find the general solution to the system.</li>
      <li>Determine whether the solution set is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^6</m>.  
          If it is, state its dimension; if not, explain why not.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Start by assembling the coefficient matrix <m>A</m> and vector <m>\mathbf{b}</m>.  
      After forming the augmented matrix, perform row reduction carefully.  
      Identify pivot and free variables before expressing the general solution.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The system can be written as <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> with
      <m>
      A = \begin{pmatrix}
      1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 3 \amp 2 \amp 1\\
      1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1\\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0
      \end{pmatrix},
      \quad
      \mathbf{b}=
      \begin{pmatrix}
      3\\2\\1
      \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      After row reduction:
      <m>
      \left[
      \begin{array}{cccccc|c}
      1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0\\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0\\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
      \end{array}
      \right].
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      A general solution is:
      <m>
      \mathbf{x}
      =
      \begin{pmatrix} 0\\0\\1\\0\\0\\0 \end{pmatrix}
      + x_4\begin{pmatrix} 0\\0\\-1\\1\\0\\0 \end{pmatrix}
      + x_5\begin{pmatrix} 0\\-1\\0\\0\\1\\0 \end{pmatrix}
      + x_6\begin{pmatrix} -1\\0\\0\\0\\0\\1 \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      The solution set is not a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^6</m>, because it does not contain the zero vector.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
       
      From the coefficients, we obtain:
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      A =
      \begin{pmatrix}
      1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 3 \amp 2 \amp 1\\
      1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1\\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0
      \end{pmatrix},
      \qquad
      \mathbf{b}=
      \begin{pmatrix}
      3\\2\\1
      \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
        
      The augmented matrix is
      <m>
      \left[
      \begin{array}{cccccc|c}
      1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 3 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3\\
      1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2\\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
      \end{array}
      \right].
      </m>
      After row reduction:
      <m>
      \left[
      \begin{array}{cccccc|c}
      1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0\\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0\\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
      \end{array}
      \right].
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
       
      The pivot variables are <m>x_1</m>, <m>x_2</m>, and <m>x_3</m>.  
      The free variables are <m>x_4</m>, <m>x_5</m>, and <m>x_6</m>.  
      Solving gives:
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      \mathbf{x}
      =
      \begin{pmatrix} 0\\0\\1\\0\\0\\0 \end{pmatrix}
      + x_4\begin{pmatrix} 0\\0\\-1\\1\\0\\0 \end{pmatrix}
      + x_5\begin{pmatrix} 0\\-1\\0\\0\\1\\0 \end{pmatrix}
      + x_6\begin{pmatrix} -1\\0\\0\\0\\0\\1 \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
       
      Because the solution set does not include the zero vector 
      (the particular solution has a nonzero entry),  
      it is not closed under scalar multiplication.  
      Therefore, the set of solutions is <em>not</em> a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^6</m>.
    </p>

  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="newton-cooling-cake-corrected">
  <title>Newton's Law of Cooling</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Newton's law of cooling states that the time rate of change of the temperature 
      <m>T(t)</m> of a body immersed in a medium of constant temperature <m>A</m> 
      is proportional to the difference <m>A - T</m>, that is,
      <me>\dfrac{dT}{dt} = k(A - T).</me>
    </p>

    <p>
      A cake is removed from an oven at <m>210^\circ\!F</m> and left to cool in a room 
      with ambient temperature <m>70^\circ\!F</m>.  
      After 30 minutes the cake’s temperature is <m>140^\circ\!F</m>.  
      When will the temperature reach <m>100^\circ\!F</m>?
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Solve the differential equation to obtain 
      <m>T(t) = A + (T_0 - A)e^{-kt}</m>.  
      Use the known temperature at <m>t=30</m> to compute <m>k</m>,  
      then solve <m>T(t)=100</m> for <m>t</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The cake reaches <m>100^\circ\!F</m> after approximately 
      <m>66.7</m> minutes (about 66 minutes and 40 seconds).
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The general solution to Newton’s cooling equation is
      <m>
        T(t) = A + (T_0 - A)e^{-kt}.
      </m>
      Using <m>A = 70</m> and <m>T_0 = 210</m>, we obtain
      <m>
        T(t) = 70 + 140e^{-kt}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      We know that <m>T(30) = 140</m>, so:
      <m>
        140 = 70 + 140e^{-30k}
        \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
        70 = 140e^{-30k}
        \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
        \tfrac12 = e^{-30k}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Taking natural logarithms,
      <m>
        -30k = \ln\!\left(\tfrac12\right) = -\ln 2,
        \quad\text{so}\quad
        k = \frac{\ln 2}{30}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Now solve for <m>t</m> when <m>T(t)=100</m>:
      <m>
        100 = 70 + 140e^{-kt}
        \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
        30 = 140e^{-kt}
        \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
        \tfrac{3}{14} = e^{-kt}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Taking logarithms again,
      <m>
        -kt = \ln\!\left(\tfrac{3}{14}\right),
        \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
        t = \frac{-\ln(\frac{3}{14})}{k}
        = \frac{-\ln(\frac{3}{14})}{\ln 2 / 30}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Numerically,
      <m>
        -\ln\!\left(\tfrac{3}{14}\right) \approx 1.540445,
        \qquad
        k \approx 0.0231049,
      </m>
      giving
      <m>
        t \approx \frac{1.540445}{0.0231049} \approx 66.7\ \text{minutes}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, the cake cools to <m>100^\circ\!F</m> after approximately 
      <m>66</m> minutes and <m>40</m> seconds.
    </p>

  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="torricelli-draining-cylinder-corrected">
  <title>Draining a Cylindrical Tank via Torricelli's Law</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      In the case of an upright cylindrical tank with constant cross-sectional area
      <m>A</m>, Torricelli's law takes the form
      <me>\dfrac{dy}{dt} = -c\sqrt{y}</me>
      where <m>c = k/A</m>.  Here <m>y(t)</m> denotes the depth of the water in the tank at time <m>t</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      A vertical cylinder initially contains water to a depth of <m>9</m> feet, and a bottom plug 
      is removed at <m>t=0</m> hours. After 1 hour, the depth has fallen to <m>4</m> feet.
      How long does it take for the tank to drain completely?
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Separate variables and integrate to obtain
      <m>2\sqrt{y} = -ct + C.</m>
      Use <m>y(0)=9</m> and <m>y(1)=4</m> to find <m>c</m>,
      then solve for the time <m>t</m> at which <m>y(t) = 0</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The tank empties after <m>3</m> hours.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Start with the differential equation
      <m>\dfrac{dy}{dt} = -c\sqrt{y}.</m>
      Separate variables:
      <m>\dfrac{dy}{\sqrt{y}} = -c\,dt.</m>
      Integrating gives
      <m>2\sqrt{y} = -ct + C.</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Use the initial condition <m>y(0) = 9</m>:
      <m>2\sqrt{9} = 6 = C.</m>
      So the solution becomes
      <m>2\sqrt{y} = -ct + 6.</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Apply the information <m>y(1)=4</m>:
      <m>
        2\sqrt{4} = 4 = -c(1) + 6
        \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
        c = 2.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus the solution is
      <m>2\sqrt{y} = -2t + 6.</m>
      The tank is empty when <m>y=0</m>, i.e., when <m>\sqrt{y}=0</m>:
      <m>
        0 = -t + 3,
        \qquad t = 3.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, the tank drains completely after <m>3</m> hours.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="mixing-tank-when-full">
  <title>Salt in a Tank with Unequal Inflow and Outflow</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      A 120-gallon tank initially contains <m>90</m> lb of salt dissolved in 
      <m>90</m> gallons of water.  
      Brine containing <m>2</m> lb/gal of salt flows into the tank at 
      <m>4</m> gal/min, and the well-stirred mixture flows out at 
      <m>3</m> gal/min.  
      How much salt does the tank contain at the moment it becomes full?
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Let <m>Q(t)</m> be the amount of salt (in lb) and 
      <m>V(t)=90+t</m> the volume (in gal).  
      Set up the differential equation
      <m>
        \dfrac{dQ}{dt} = 8 \;-\; 3\,\dfrac{Q}{V(t)}.
      </m>
      Solve the resulting linear differential equation and then evaluate 
      <m>Q(t)</m> at the time the tank reaches <m>120</m> gallons.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      When the tank is full, it contains approximately 
      <m>202.03</m> lb of salt.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Since the inflow is <m>4</m> gal/min and the outflow is <m>3</m> gal/min, 
      the tank gains volume at <m>1</m> gal/min.  
      Starting at 90 gallons, it reaches its 120-gallon capacity after 
      <m>30</m> minutes.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>Q(t)</m> be the amount of salt in pounds.  
      The inflow brings in <m>4 \times 2 = 8</m> lb/min.  
      The outflow concentration is <m>Q(t)/V(t)</m> where 
      <m>V(t)=90+t</m>.  
      Thus the outflow removes 
      <m>3\,Q(t)/(90+t)</m> lb/min.
    </p>

    <p>
      The differential equation is therefore
      <m>
        \dfrac{dQ}{dt} \;=\; 8 \;-\; 3\,\dfrac{Q}{90+t}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      This is linear:
      <m>
        \dfrac{dQ}{dt} + \dfrac{3}{90+t}Q = 8.
      </m>
      The integrating factor is
      <m>
        \mu(t) = (90+t)^3.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Multiplying and integrating gives
      <m>
        (90+t)^3 Q = 2(90+t)^4 + C.
      </m>
      Using <m>Q(0)=90</m>:
      <m>
        90^3 \cdot 90 = 2\cdot 90^4 + C
        \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
        C = -90^4.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus
      <m>
        Q(t) = 2(90+t) \;-\; \dfrac{90^4}{(90+t)^3}.
      </m>
      At <m>t=30</m> (when the tank is full), this gives
      <m>
        Q(30) = 2(120)\;-\;\dfrac{90^4}{120^3}
        \approx 202.03\ \text{lb}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, the tank contains approximately 
      <m>202.03</m> lb of salt when it becomes full.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="linear-system-k-values">
  <title>Classifying Solutions of a Linear System</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Determine for which values of <m>k</m> the system
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      \begin{aligned}
      3x + 2y \amp= 1,\\
      6x + 4y \amp= k
      \end{aligned}
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      has
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>a unique solution,</li>
      <li>no solution,</li>
      <li>infinitely many solutions.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Compare the two equations.  
      Notice that the left-hand side of the second equation is 
      <m>2</m> times the left-hand side of the first equation.
      Check whether the right-hand sides show the same relationship.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The system has:
      <ul>
        <li><em>no unique solution</em> for any value of <m>k</m>,</li>
        <li><em>no solution</em> when <m>k \ne 2</m>,</li>
        <li><em>infinitely many solutions</em> when <m>k = 2</m>.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The coefficient matrix is
      <m>
      \begin{pmatrix}
      3 \amp 2\\
      6 \amp 4
      \end{pmatrix},
      </m>
      whose determinant is
      <m>
      3\cdot 4 - 6\cdot 2 = 12 - 12 = 0.
      </m>
      Therefore, the system is never invertible and can never have a unique solution.
    </p>

    <p>
      Observe that the left-hand side of the second equation is exactly twice the 
      first equation's left-hand side:
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      6x + 4y = 2(3x + 2y).
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      For the system to be consistent, the right-hand sides must also satisfy the same relationship:
      <m>
      k = 2 \cdot 1 = 2.
      </m>
      Thus, when <m>k = 2</m>, the second equation is a duplicate of the first, and the system 
      has infinitely many solutions.
    </p>

    <p>
      When <m>k \ne 2</m>, the second equation contradicts the first, so the system has no solution.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore:
      <ul>
        <li>No unique solution for any value of <m>k</m>,</li>
        <li>No solution when <m>k \ne 2</m>,</li>
        <li>Infinitely many solutions when <m>k = 2</m>.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="matrix-binomial-failure">
  <title>Testing the Matrix Binomial Identity</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose
      <m>
        A = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \amp -1 \\ 4 \amp -3 \end{pmatrix}
      </m>
      and
      <m>
        B = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 3 \\ 5 \amp 7 \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
      Does the identity
      <m>(A+B)^2 = A^2 + 2AB + B^2?</m>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Compute <m>(A+B)^2</m> explicitly and compare it to 
      <m>A^2 + 2AB + B^2</m>.  
      Remember that matrix multiplication is not commutative, so 
      <m>AB \ne BA</m> in general.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      No.  The two matrices are not equal, so the binomial identity does not hold
      for these matrices.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      First compute
      <m>
        A+B = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \amp 2 \\ 9 \amp 4 \end{pmatrix},
      </m>
      so
      <m>
        (A+B)^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 27 \amp 14 \\ 63 \amp 34 \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Next compute the pieces on the right side:
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      A^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 6 \amp -1 \\ -8 \amp -5 \end{pmatrix}, \quad
      AB = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \amp -1 \\ -11 \amp -9 \end{pmatrix}, \quad
      2AB = \begin{pmatrix} -6 \amp -2 \\ -22 \amp -18 \end{pmatrix},
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      B^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 16 \amp 24 \\ 40 \amp 64 \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Adding these,
      <m>
        A^2 + 2AB + B^2
        =
        \begin{pmatrix}
          16 \amp 21\\
          10 \amp 41
        \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Comparing,
      <m>
        (A+B)^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 27 \amp 14 \\ 63 \amp 34 \end{pmatrix}
      </m>
      is not equal to
      <m>
        A^2 + 2AB + B^2.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, the identity does not hold.  
      This occurs because the binomial identity <m>(A+B)^2 = A^2 + 2AB + B^2</m>
      only holds when <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> commute (i.e., <m>AB = BA</m>), which is not the case here.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="inverse-by-row-reduction-correct">
  <title>Finding a Matrix Inverse by Row Reduction</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find <m>A^{-1}</m> if
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{pmatrix}
          1 \amp 1 \amp 5\\
          1 \amp 4 \amp 13\\
          3 \amp 2 \amp 12
        \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Form the augmented matrix <m>[A \mid I]</m> and row‑reduce until the left block
      becomes the identity matrix.  
      The right block will then be <m>A^{-1}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
      A^{-1} =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        -22 \amp 2 \amp 7\\
        -27 \amp 3 \amp 8\\
        10 \amp -1 \amp -3
      \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Begin with the augmented matrix <m>[A \mid I]</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
      \left[
      \begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
      1 \amp 1 \amp 5 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0\\
      1 \amp 4 \amp 13 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0\\
      3 \amp 2 \amp 12 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
      \end{array}
      \right]
      </m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Row reduction yields:
      <m>
      \left[
      \begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
      1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -22 \amp 2 \amp 7\\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -27 \amp 3 \amp 8\\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 10 \amp -1 \amp -3
      \end{array}
      \right].
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore,
      <m>
      A^{-1} =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        -22 \amp 2 \amp 7\\
        -27 \amp 3 \amp 8\\
        10 \amp -1 \amp -3
      \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
      Multiplying <m>A A^{-1}</m> confirms that the product is the identity matrix.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="tridiagonal-determinant">
  <title>Determinants of Tridiagonal Matrices</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the determinants:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        Compute
        <m>
        \det\begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 1\\ 1 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}
        </m>
        and
        <m>
        \det\begin{pmatrix}
        2 \amp 1 \amp 0\\
        1 \amp 2 \amp 1\\
        0 \amp 1 \amp 2
        \end{pmatrix}.
        </m>
      </li>

      <li>
        Let
        <me>
        B_n = 
        \det\!
        \begin{pmatrix}
        2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp 0\\
        1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp 0\\
        0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp 0\\
        0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp 0\\
        \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \amp \vdots\\
        0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 2 \amp 1\\
        0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 1 \amp 2
        \end{pmatrix}
        </me>
        where all main diagonal entries are 2, the adjacent diagonals are 1, and all other entries are 0.
        Show that
        <m>
        B_n = 2B_{n-1} - B_{n-2}.
        </m>
      </li>

      <li>
        Give an explicit formula for <m>B_n</m> for <m>n \ge 2</m>.
      </li>
    </ol>

  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      For (a), expand the determinants directly.
      For (b), perform a cofactor expansion along the first row: only two minors survive.
      For (c), solve the linear recurrence 
      <m>B_n = 2B_{n-1} - B_{n-2}</m> 
      using the initial conditions from part (a).
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <m>\det\begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 1\\ 1 \amp 2\end{pmatrix} = 3</m>,  
          <m>\det\begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 1 \amp 0\\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 1\\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2\end{pmatrix} = 4</m>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <m>B_n = 2B_{n-1} - B_{n-2}</m>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <m>B_n = n + 1</m>.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      <strong>(a)</strong>  
      The <m>2 \times 2</m> determinant is
      <m>
      2\cdot 2 - 1\cdot 1 = 3.
      </m>
      For the <m>3 \times 3</m> determinant, expand along the first row:
      <m>
      2\det\begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 1\\ 1 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}
      - \det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 1\\ 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}
      = 2(3) - 2 = 4.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(b)</strong>  
      Expanding <m>B_n</m> along the first row produces only two terms:
      <m>
      B_n = 2 B_{n-1} - B_{n-2}.
      </m>
      The sign pattern in the cofactor expansion ensures the subtraction of the second minor.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(c)</strong>  
      The recurrence
      <m>B_n = 2B_{n-1} - B_{n-2}</m>
      has characteristic equation
      <m>(r-1)^2=0</m>,
      giving
      <m>B_n = C_1 + C_2 n.</m>
      Using
      <m>B_1 = 2</m>
      and
      <m>B_2 = 3</m>
      yields
      <m>C_2 = 1</m> and <m>C_1 = 1</m>.
      Thus
      <m>
      B_n = n + 1.
      </m>
      This agrees with <m>B_3 = 4</m> from part (a).
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="linear-dependence-check">
  <title>Checking Linear Dependence of Three Vectors</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Determine whether the vectors
      <m>\begin{pmatrix}3\\ -1\\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m>,
      <m>\begin{pmatrix}5\\ 4\\ -6\end{pmatrix}</m>, and
      <m>\begin{pmatrix}8\\ 3\\ -4\end{pmatrix}</m>
      are linearly dependent or linearly independent.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Form a matrix whose columns are the three vectors and compute its determinant.
      A determinant of zero means the vectors are linearly dependent.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The three vectors are <em>linearly dependent</em>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Form the matrix
      <m>
      M =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        3 \amp 5 \amp 8\\
        -1 \amp 4 \amp 3\\
        2 \amp -6 \amp -4
      \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
      Compute its determinant:
      <m>
      \det(M)
      =
      3\begin{vmatrix}4 \amp 3\\ -6 \amp -4\end{vmatrix}
      -
      5\begin{vmatrix}-1 \amp 3\\ 2 \amp -4\end{vmatrix}
      +
      8\begin{vmatrix}-1 \amp 4\\ 2 \amp -6\end{vmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Evaluating the minors:
      <m>
      \begin{aligned}
      \begin{vmatrix}4 \amp 3\\ -6 \amp -4\end{vmatrix} \amp = 2,\\[4pt]
      \begin{vmatrix}-1 \amp 3\\ 2 \amp -4\end{vmatrix} \amp = -2,\\[4pt]
      \begin{vmatrix}-1 \amp 4\\ 2 \amp -6\end{vmatrix} \amp = -2.
      \end{aligned}
      </m>
      Thus
      <m>
      \det(M) = 3(2) - 5(-2) + 8(-2) = 6 + 10 - 16 = 0.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Since the determinant is zero, the matrix is singular, and therefore the three
      vectors do not form a linearly independent set.
    </p>

    <p>
      Hence, the vectors are <em>linearly dependent</em>.
    </p>

  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="basis-subspace-x1+x2+x3=0">
  <title>Finding a Basis for a Subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^{3}</m></title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find a basis for the subspace
      <m>
        S = \left\{
        \begin{pmatrix} x_1\\ x_2\\ x_3 \end{pmatrix}
        : x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = 0
        \right\}
      </m>
      of <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.  Compute <m>\dim(S)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Solve the equation <m>x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = 0</m> for one variable in terms of the
      other two, and express all vectors in <m>S</m> as linear combinations of
      two independent vectors.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      A possible basis is
      <m>
      \left\{
      \begin{pmatrix}-1\\ 1\\ 0\end{pmatrix},
      \begin{pmatrix}-1\\ 0\\ 1\end{pmatrix}
      \right\},
      </m>
      and <m>\dim(S)=2</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The subspace <m>S</m> consists of all vectors
      <m>
      \begin{pmatrix} x_1\\ x_2\\ x_3 \end{pmatrix}
      </m>
      satisfying the linear constraint
      <m>x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = 0.</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Solve this equation for <m>x_1</m>:
      <m>
      x_1 = -x_2 - x_3.
      </m>
      Then every vector in <m>S</m> can be written as
      <m>
      \begin{pmatrix}
        -x_2 - x_3\\
        x_2\\
        x_3
      \end{pmatrix}
      =
      x_2 \begin{pmatrix}-1\\1\\0\end{pmatrix}
      +
      x_3 \begin{pmatrix}-1\\0\\1\end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      The vectors
      <m>
      \begin{pmatrix}-1\\1\\0\end{pmatrix}
      </m>
      and
      <m>
      \begin{pmatrix}-1\\0\\1\end{pmatrix}
      </m>
      are linearly independent, so they form a basis for <m>S</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Since the basis contains two independent vectors,
      <m>\dim(S)=2.</m>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="nullspace-of-A-corrected">
  <title>Finding a Basis for a Null Space</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find a basis for the null space of
      <m>
      A =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        1 \amp 3 \amp 8 \amp -1\\
        1 \amp -3 \amp -10 \amp 5\\
        1 \amp 4 \amp 11 \amp -2
      \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Row-reduce the augmented matrix <m>[A \mid \mathbf{0}]</m> and express the solution
      in terms of the free variables.  The resulting parameter vectors form a basis.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      A basis for the null space is
      <m>
      \left\{
      \begin{pmatrix}1\\ -3\\ 1\\ 0\end{pmatrix},
      \begin{pmatrix}-2\\ 1\\ 0\\ 1\end{pmatrix}
      \right\}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Row-reducing the matrix <m>[A \mid \mathbf{0}]</m> yields
      <m>
      \begin{pmatrix}
        1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 2\\
        0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp -1\\
        0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
      \end{pmatrix},
      </m>
      corresponding to the equations
      <m>
        x_1 - x_3 + 2x_4 = 0, \qquad
        x_2 + 3x_3 - x_4 = 0.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>x_3 = s</m> and <m>x_4 = t</m>. Then
      <m>
      x_1 = s - 2t,\qquad
      x_2 = -3s + t.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      The general solution is
      <m>
      \mathbf{x}
      =
      s\begin{pmatrix}1\\ -3\\ 1\\ 0\end{pmatrix}
      +
      t\begin{pmatrix}-2\\ 1\\ 0\\ 1\end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, a basis for the null space is
      <m>
      \left\{
      \begin{pmatrix}1\\ -3\\ 1\\ 0\end{pmatrix},
      \begin{pmatrix}-2\\ 1\\ 0\\ 1\end{pmatrix}
      \right\}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="linear-combination-matrix-equation">
  <title>Finding a Nontrivial Linear Combination</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find a nontrivial linear combination of the vectors
      <m>\left[ \begin{array}{c} 2\\0\\-3 \end{array} \right]</m>,
      <m>\left[ \begin{array}{c} 4\\-5\\6 \end{array} \right]</m>, and
      <m>\left[ \begin{array}{c} -2\\1\\3 \end{array} \right]</m>
      that equals the zero vector.  
      Then determine whether the vectors are linearly dependent or linearly independent.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Form the matrix with the vectors as columns:
      <m>
      M =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        2 \amp 4 \amp -2\\
        0 \amp -5 \amp 1\\
        -3 \amp 6 \amp 3
      \end{pmatrix},
      </m>
      and solve the homogeneous system <m>M\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{0}</m>.
      Any nonzero solution <m>\mathbf{c}</m> gives a dependence relation.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      One nontrivial dependence relation is
      <m>
      3\left[ \begin{array}{c}2\\0\\-3\end{array}\right]
      +
      1\left[ \begin{array}{c}4\\-5\\6\end{array}\right]
      +
      5\left[ \begin{array}{c}-2\\1\\3\end{array}\right]
      =
      \left[ \begin{array}{c}0\\0\\0\end{array}\right].
      </m>
      Thus, the vectors are linearly dependent.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Place the vectors as columns in the matrix
      <m>
      M =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        2 \amp 4 \amp -2\\
        0 \amp -5 \amp 1\\
        -3 \amp 6 \amp 3
      \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
      We seek a nonzero vector <m>\mathbf{c}</m> such that <m>M\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{0}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Solving the homogeneous system by row reduction yields a one-parameter family
      of solutions.  One convenient choice is
      <m>
      \mathbf{c} =
      \begin{bmatrix} 3\\ 1\\ 5 \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Substituting back:
      <m>
      3\left[ \begin{array}{c}2\\0\\-3\end{array}\right]
      +
      1\left[ \begin{array}{c}4\\-5\\6\end{array}\right]
      +
      5\left[ \begin{array}{c}-2\\1\\3\end{array}\right]
      =
      \left[ \begin{array}{c}
      0\\0\\0
      \end{array}\right].
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Since a nontrivial linear combination equals the zero vector, the three vectors
      are <em>linearly dependent</em>.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="basis-check-R4">
  <title>Do Three Vectors Form a Basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^{4}</m>?</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Determine whether the vectors
      <m>\left[ \begin{array}{c} 2\\0\\-3\\1 \end{array} \right]</m>,
      <m>\left[ \begin{array}{c} 4\\-5\\6\\-3 \end{array} \right]</m>, and
      <m>\left[ \begin{array}{c} -2\\1\\3\\0 \end{array} \right]</m>
      form a basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m>.  
      If not, explain why.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Three vectors cannot form a basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m>, because any basis
      must contain four linearly independent vectors.  
      But you may still check whether these three vectors are linearly independent.
      Form the matrix
      <m>
      M =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        2 \amp 4 \amp -2\\
        0 \amp -5 \amp 1\\
        -3 \amp 6 \amp 3\\
        1 \amp -3 \amp 0
      \end{pmatrix}
      </m>
      and solve <m>M\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{0}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The vectors do <em>not</em> form a basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m> because a basis
      requires four vectors.  
      However, these three vectors are linearly independent.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      A basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m> must contain exactly four linearly independent
      vectors. Since only three vectors are given, they cannot form a basis.
    </p>

    <p>
      To check linear independence, place the vectors as columns of the matrix
      <m>
      M =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        2 \amp 4 \amp -2\\
        0 \amp -5 \amp 1\\
        -3 \amp 6 \amp 3\\
        1 \amp -3 \amp 0
      \end{pmatrix}
      </m>
      and solve the homogeneous system <m>M\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{0}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Row reduction yields only the trivial solution. Thus the three vectors are
      linearly independent.
    </p>

    <p>
      Despite being independent, they do <em>not</em> form a basis for 
      <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m> because there are only three of them.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="solution-space-yprime-equals-ky">
  <title>The Solution Space of <m>y' = ky</m></title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Determine whether the set of all solutions to the differential equation
      <m>y' = ky</m> forms a vector space.  
      If it does, give a basis for this function space.  
      If it does not, explain why.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Solve the differential equation first.  
      Then check whether the set of all solutions is closed under addition and 
      scalar multiplication, and whether it contains the zero function.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      Yes, the solution set is a vector space.  
      A basis is <m>\{ e^{kt} \}</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Solving the differential equation <m>y' = ky</m> gives the family of 
      solutions
      <m>
        y(t) = Ce^{kt},
      </m>
      where <m>C</m> is any real constant.
    </p>

    <p>
      The set of solutions is therefore
      <m>
        S = \{ Ce^{kt} : C \in \mathbb{R} \}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      This set is closed under addition:
      <m>
      C_1 e^{kt} + C_2 e^{kt} = (C_1 + C_2)e^{kt} \in S.
      </m>
      It is closed under scalar multiplication:
      <m>
      \alpha (Ce^{kt}) = (\alpha C)e^{kt} \in S.
      </m>
      And it contains the zero function by choosing <m>C = 0</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, <m>S</m> is a vector space.  
      Since every element of <m>S</m> is a scalar multiple of <m>e^{kt}</m>, 
      a basis is
      <m>\{ e^{kt} \}</m>,
      and the solution space is one-dimensional.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="dimension-solution-space-y-triple-prime">
  <title>Dimension of the Solution Space to <m>y''' = 0</m></title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose <m>S</m> is the solution space to the differential equation
      <m>y''' = 0</m>.  
      Compute <m>\dim(S)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Integrate the equation three times to obtain the general form of all solutions.
      Then determine how many independent functions appear.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>\dim(S) = 3</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Integrating <m>y''' = 0</m> yields
      <m>y'' = C_1</m>,
      <m>y' = C_1 t + C_2</m>, and finally
      <m>y = C_1 t^2 + C_2 t + C_3</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus every solution is a linear combination of the three functions
      <m>t^2</m>, <m>t</m>, and <m>1</m>.  
      These three functions are linearly independent.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, the dimension of the solution space is
      <m>\dim(S) = 3</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="subspaces-of-P3">
  <title>Subspaces of <m>\mathbb{P}_{3}</m></title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the set of all polynomials of the form
      <m>a_0 + a_1 x + a_2 x^2 + a_3 x^3</m>.
      Determine whether the set of all such polynomials satisfying each condition
      below is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{P}_3</m>.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li><m>a_3 \ne 0</m></li>
      <li><m>a_0 = a_1 = 0</m></li>
      <li><m>a_0 + a_1 + a_2 + a_3 = 0</m></li>
      <li>All coefficients <m>a_0, a_1, a_2, a_3</m> are integers</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Recall that a subspace must contain the zero polynomial and must be closed
      under both addition and scalar multiplication.  
      For (c), notice that a single homogeneous linear equation defines a subspace.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>(a) Not a subspace.</li>
        <li>(b) A subspace.</li>
        <li>(c) A subspace.</li>
        <li>(d) Not a subspace.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      <strong>(a)</strong>  
      The condition <m>a_3 \ne 0</m> excludes the zero polynomial, so the set
      cannot be a subspace.  It is also not closed under scalar multiplication.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(b)</strong>  
      The condition <m>a_0 = a_1 = 0</m> gives polynomials of the form
      <m>a_2 x^2 + a_3 x^3</m>, which is closed under addition and scalar
      multiplication and contains the zero polynomial.  
      Thus it is a subspace.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(c)</strong>  
      The condition <m>a_0 + a_1 + a_2 + a_3 = 0</m> is a single homogeneous
      linear constraint on the coefficients.  Such a set is always a subspace.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(d)</strong>  
      Requiring all coefficients to be integers makes the set not closed under
      scalar multiplication (for example, multiply any such polynomial by
      <m>\tfrac12</m>).  Therefore it is not a subspace.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="odd-functions-subspace">
  <title>Odd Functions as a Subspace</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Is the set of all real-valued functions <m>f</m> satisfying
      <m>f(-x) = -f(x)</m> for all real <m>x</m> a subspace of the vector space
      of all real-valued functions on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>?
      If so, provide a basis for this function space.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Check whether the set contains the zero function and is closed under
      addition and scalar multiplication.  Use the identity
      <m>(f+g)(-x) = f(-x) + g(-x)</m> and 
      <m>(cf)(-x) = c f(-x)</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      Yes, the set of all odd functions is a subspace.  
      A possible basis is <m>\{x, x^3, x^5, \dots\}</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The condition <m>f(-x) = -f(x)</m> defines the set of odd functions.
      The zero function satisfies <m>0(-x) = 0 = -0(x)</m>, so it is in the set.
    </p>

    <p>
      If <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are odd, then
      <m>
      (f+g)(-x) = f(-x) + g(-x)
      = -f(x) - g(x)
      = -(f(x) + g(x)),
      </m>
      so <m>f+g</m> is also odd.
    </p>

    <p>
      For any scalar <m>c</m>,
      <m>
      (cf)(-x) = c f(-x) = c(-f(x)) = -(cf)(x),
      </m>
      so the set is closed under scalar multiplication.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, the odd functions form a subspace of the vector space of all
      real-valued functions on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.  
      A basis for this space is, for example,
      <m>\{x, x^3, x^5, x^7, \dots\}</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="general-solution-ypp-3yp-2y">
  <title>General Solution to a Second-Order Linear Homogeneous DE</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the general solution to the differential equation
      <m>y'' - 3y' + 2y = 0</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Try a solution of the form <m>y = e^{rt}</m> and substitute into the
      differential equation to obtain the characteristic equation.
      Then solve for the roots <m>r</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>y(t) = C_1 e^{t} + C_2 e^{2t}</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Substitute <m>y = e^{rt}</m> into the differential equation
      <m>y'' - 3y' + 2y = 0</m>.
      This gives the characteristic equation
      <m>
        r^2 - 3r + 2 = 0.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Factor the quadratic:
      <m>
        r^2 - 3r + 2 = (r - 1)(r - 2).
      </m>
      Thus, the roots are <m>r = 1</m> and <m>r = 2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Since the characteristic equation has two distinct real roots, the
      general solution is
      <m>
        y(t) = C_1 e^{t} + C_2 e^{2t},
      </m>
      where <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are arbitrary constants.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="general-solution-2ypp-3yp">
  <title>General Solution to a Linear Homogeneous DE</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the general solution to the differential equation
      <m>2y'' + 3y' = 0</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Divide the equation by <m>2</m> and try a solution of the form 
      <m>y = e^{rt}</m>.  
      This produces the characteristic equation
      <m>r(r + \tfrac{3}{2}) = 0</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>y(t) = C_1 + C_2 e^{-3t/2}</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Rewrite the differential equation as
      <m>
        y'' + \tfrac{3}{2} y' = 0.
      </m>
      Using the trial solution <m>y = e^{rt}</m> gives the characteristic
      equation
      <m>
        r^2 + \tfrac{3}{2}r = 0.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Factor:
      <m>
        r(r + \tfrac{3}{2}) = 0,
      </m>
      so the roots are <m>r_1 = 0</m> and <m>r_2 = -\tfrac{3}{2}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      With two distinct real roots, the general solution is
      <m>
        y(t) = C_1 e^{0t} + C_2 e^{-3t/2}
        = C_1 + C_2 e^{-3t/2}.
      </m>
    </p>

  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="general-solution-9ypp-12yp-4y">
  <title>General Solution to a Second-Order Linear DE</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the general solution to the differential equation
      <m>9y'' - 12y' + 4y = 0</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Use the trial solution <m>y = e^{rt}</m> to obtain the characteristic
      equation.  Check whether the equation has distinct, repeated, or complex
      roots.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
      y(t) = (C_1 + C_2 t)e^{\frac{2}{3} t}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Substitute <m>y = e^{rt}</m> into the DE to obtain the characteristic
      equation
      <m>
      9r^2 - 12r + 4 = 0.
      </m>
      Compute the discriminant:
      <m>
      (-12)^2 - 4(9)(4) = 144 - 144 = 0.
      </m>
      Since it is zero, there is a repeated root.
    </p>

    <p>
      Solving,
      <m>
      r = \frac{12}{18} = \frac{2}{3}.
      </m>
      For a repeated root <m>r</m>, the general solution is
      <m>
      y(t) = (C_1 + C_2 t)e^{rt}.
      </m>
      Therefore,
      <m>
      y(t) = (C_1 + C_2 t)e^{\frac{2}{3} t}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="find-DE-from-general-solution">
  <title>Finding a Differential Equation from Its General Solution</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      In each of the following, you are given a general solution <m>y(x)</m>
      of a homogeneous second-order differential equation
      <m>ay'' + by' + cy = 0</m> with constant coefficients.
      Find such an equation.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li><m>y(x) = c_1 + c_2 e^{-10x}</m></li>
      <li><m>y(x) = c_1 e^{10x} + c_2 e^{-10x}</m></li>
      <li><m>y(x) = c_1 e^{-10x} + c_2 x e^{-10x}</m></li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Identify the characteristic roots from the form of the general solution.
      For distinct roots <m>r_1, r_2</m>, the characteristic equation is
      <m>(r - r_1)(r - r_2) = 0</m>.
      For repeated roots <m>r</m>, the solution has the form
      <m>(c_1 + c_2 x)e^{rx}</m> and the characteristic equation is
      <m>(r - r)^2 = 0</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li><m>y'' + 10y' = 0</m></li>
        <li><m>y'' - 100y = 0</m></li>
        <li><m>y'' + 20y' + 100y = 0</m></li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      <strong>(a)</strong>
      The solution contains <m>e^{0x}</m> and <m>e^{-10x}</m>, so the characteristic
      roots are <m>r_1 = 0</m> and <m>r_2 = -10</m>.  
      Thus,
      <m>(r)(r+10) = r^2 + 10r = 0</m>.
      The DE is <m>y'' + 10y' = 0</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(b)</strong>
      The roots are <m>r = 10</m> and <m>r = -10</m>.  
      The characteristic equation is
      <m>(r-10)(r+10) = r^2 - 100 = 0</m>.
      Hence the DE is <m>y'' - 100y = 0</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(c)</strong>
      The presence of <m>x e^{-10x}</m> shows a repeated root <m>r = -10</m>.
      Thus,
      <m>(r+10)^2 = r^2 + 20r + 100 = 0</m>.
      The DE is <m>y'' + 20y' + 100y = 0</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="general-solution-5y4p-3y3p">
  <title>General Solution of a Fourth-Order Linear DE</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the general solution to the differential equation
      <m>5y'''' + 3y''' = 0</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Try a solution of the form <m>y = e^{rx}</m> to obtain the characteristic
      equation.  Factor it to find the roots and their multiplicities.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
      y(x) = C_1 + C_2 x + C_3 x^2 + C_4 e^{-3x/5}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Substitute <m>y = e^{rx}</m> into the differential equation
      <m>5y'''' + 3y''' = 0</m> to obtain the characteristic equation
      <m>5r^4 + 3r^3 = 0.</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Factor:
      <m>
      r^3(5r + 3) = 0.
      </m>
      Thus the roots are <m>r = 0</m> (with multiplicity 3) and
      <m>r = -\frac{3}{5}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For a triple root at <m>r = 0</m>, the corresponding solutions are
      <m>1</m>, <m>x</m>, and <m>x^2</m>.  
      For the simple root <m>r = -\frac{3}{5}</m>, the solution is
      <m>e^{-3x/5}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, the general solution is
      <m>
      y(x) = C_1 + C_2 x + C_3 x^2 + C_4 e^{-3x/5}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="general-solution-ypp-8yp-25y">
  <title>General Solution to a Homogeneous Linear DE</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the general solution to the differential equation
      <m>y'' + 8y' + 25y = 0</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Use the trial solution <m>y = e^{rt}</m> to obtain the characteristic
      equation.  If the roots are complex, recall that solutions have the form
      <m>e^{\alpha t}(C_1\cos(\beta t) + C_2\sin(\beta t))</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
      y(t) = e^{-4t}\left(C_1 \cos(3t) + C_2 \sin(3t)\right).
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Substitute <m>y = e^{rt}</m> into <m>y'' + 8y' + 25y = 0</m> to obtain the
      characteristic equation
      <m>r^2 + 8r + 25 = 0.</m>
      The discriminant is
      <m>64 - 100 = -36</m>, so the roots are complex:
      <m>r = -4 \pm 3i.</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      For complex roots <m>\alpha \pm \beta i</m>, the general solution is
      <m>
        y(t) = e^{\alpha t}\left(C_1 \cos(\beta t) + C_2 \sin(\beta t)\right).
      </m>
      Using <m>\alpha = -4</m> and <m>\beta = 3</m>, we obtain
      <m>
        y(t) = e^{-4t}\left(C_1 \cos(3t) + C_2 \sin(3t)\right).
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="third-order-DE-from-basis">
  <title>Constructing a DE from a Given Solution Basis</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find a linear, constant coefficient, third-order homogeneous differential
      equation whose solution space has basis
      <m>\{ e^{-3x},\ \cos(2x),\ \sin(2x) \}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      The functions <m>e^{-3x}</m>, <m>\cos(2x)</m>, and <m>\sin(2x)</m>
      correspond to characteristic roots <m>-3</m>, <m>2i</m>, and <m>-2i</m>.
      Form the characteristic polynomial with these roots and expand it.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      One such differential equation is
      <m>
        y''' + 3y'' + 4y' + 12y = 0.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The given basis corresponds to characteristic roots
      <m>-3</m>, <m>2i</m>, and <m>-2i</m>.  
      Therefore the characteristic equation is
      <m>
        (r + 3)(r^2 + 4) = 0.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Expanding,
      <m>
        (r+3)(r^2+4) = r^3 + 3r^2 + 4r + 12.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Replacing <m>r^k</m> with <m>y^{(k)}</m>, the corresponding differential
      equation is
      <m>
        y''' + 3y'' + 4y' + 12y = 0.
      </m>
      This equation has exactly the desired basis of solutions.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="mass-spring-amplitude-period-frequency">
  <title>Amplitude, Period, and Frequency of a Mass–Spring System</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      A mass of <m>3</m> kg is attached to the end of a spring that is stretched
      <m>20</m> cm by a force of <m>15</m> N.  
      It is set in motion with initial position <m>x_0 = 0</m> and
      initial velocity <m>v_0 = -10</m> m/s.
      Find the amplitude, period, and frequency of the resulting motion.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Use Hooke’s law to compute the spring constant <m>k</m>.
      Then solve the differential equation <m>mx'' + kx = 0</m>.
      Apply the initial conditions to determine the constants and extract the
      amplitude, period, and frequency.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      Amplitude: <m>2</m> m;  
      Period: <m>\tfrac{2\pi}{5}</m>;  
      Frequency: <m>\tfrac{5}{2\pi}</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      From Hooke’s law, <m>k = F/\Delta x = 15/0.2 = 75</m>.  
      The motion satisfies <m>3x'' + 75x = 0</m>, or <m>x'' + 25x = 0</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The general solution is
      <m>
        x(t) = C_1 \cos(5t) + C_2 \sin(5t),
      </m>
      since <m>\omega = \sqrt{25} = 5</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Using <m>x(0)=0</m> gives <m>C_1 = 0</m>.  
      Differentiating:
      <m>x'(t) = 5C_2 \cos(5t).</m>
      Applying <m>x'(0) = -10</m> yields <m>C_2 = -2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore the solution is
      <m>x(t) = -2\sin(5t).</m>
      The amplitude is <m>A = 2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The period is
      <m>
        T = \frac{2\pi}{\omega} = \frac{2\pi}{5},
      </m>
      and the frequency is
      <m>
        f = \frac{1}{T} = \frac{5}{2\pi}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="particular-solution-xppp-4xp">
  <title>Finding a Particular Solution</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find a particular solution <m>x_p(t)</m> of the differential equation
      <m>x''' + 4x' = 3t - 1</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Because the right-hand side is a polynomial of degree 1, try a polynomial
      particular solution of degree 2: <m>x_p(t) = At^2 + Bt + C</m>.  
      Compute the required derivatives and match coefficients.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
      x_p(t) = \frac{3}{8} t^2 - \frac14 t.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Try a particular solution of the form <m>x_p(t) = At^2 + Bt + C</m>.
      Then
      <m>
      x_p'(t) = 2At + B,\quad
      x_p''(t) = 2A,\quad
      x_p'''(t) = 0.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Substitute into the differential equation:
      <m>
      0 + 4(2At + B) = 3t - 1,
      </m>
      so
      <m>
      8At + 4B = 3t - 1.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Matching coefficients gives
      <m>8A = 3</m> and <m>4B = -1</m>, so
      <m>
      A = \frac{3}{8}, B = -\frac14.
      </m>
      The constant <m>C</m> does not appear, so we may take <m>C=0</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, a particular solution is
      <m>
      x_p(t) = \frac{3}{8} t^2 - \frac14 t.
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="undetermined-coefficients-forms">
  <title>Forms of Particular Solutions via Undetermined Coefficients</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      This problem uses the method of undetermined coefficients.  
      For each differential equation below, write an appropriate form of a
      particular solution <m>y_p(x)</m>.  
      <em>Do not solve for the coefficients.</em>
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li><m>y'' - 2y' + 2y = e^{x}\sin x</m></li>
      <li><m>y''''' - y''' = e^{x} + 2x^2 - 5</m></li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Match the form of the right-hand side with the standard trial forms used in
      the method of undetermined coefficients.  
      If any part of the trial solution overlaps with the complementary solution,
      multiply by a sufficient power of <m>x</m> to remove the overlap.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li><m>y_p(x) = x e^{x}(A\cos x + B\sin x)</m></li>
        <li><m>y_p(x) = Ax e^{x} + x^3(Bx^2 + Cx + D)</m></li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      <strong>(a)</strong>  
      The forcing term <m>e^{x}\sin x</m> suggests a trial solution of the form
      <m>e^{x}(A\cos x + B\sin x)</m>.  
      However, the complementary solution already contains the functions
      <m>e^{x}\cos x</m> and <m>e^{x}\sin x</m>, so we multiply the trial by
      <m>x</m>.  
      Thus,
      <m>y_p(x) = x e^{x}(A\cos x + B\sin x)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(b)</strong>  
      The right-hand side has two components:
      <m>e^{x}</m> and <m>2x^2 - 5</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since <m>r=1</m> is a root of the characteristic equation 
      <m>r^5 - r^3 = 0</m>, the trial for <m>e^{x}</m> must be multiplied by 
      <m>x</m>, giving <m>Ax e^{x}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the polynomial <m>2x^2 - 5</m>, one normally tries
      <m>Bx^2 + Cx + D</m>, but <m>r=0</m> is a root with multiplicity 3.
      Therefore, we multiply by <m>x^3</m>, giving
      <m>x^3(Bx^2 + Cx + D)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Combining both parts yields
      <m>
      y_p(x) = Ax e^{x} + x^3(Bx^2 + Cx + D).
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="eigenvalues-eigenspaces-A">
  <title>Eigenvalues and Eigenspaces of a Matrix</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the eigenvalues and associated eigenspaces of the matrix
      <m>
        A = 
        \begin{pmatrix}
          4 \amp -2 \amp 1\\
          2 \amp 0 \amp 1\\
          2 \amp -2 \amp 3
        \end{pmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Compute the characteristic polynomial <m>\det(A - \lambda I)</m> and factor it
      to find the eigenvalues.  
      Then solve <m>(A - \lambda I)\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{0}</m> for each eigenvalue
      to find its eigenspace.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      Eigenvalues:
      <m>\lambda = 3</m>,  
      <m>\lambda = 2</m> (multiplicity 2).  
      Corresponding eigenspaces:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <m>E_3 = \operatorname{span}\left\{
          \left[\begin{array}{c}1\\1\\1\end{array}\right]\right\}</m>
        </li>
        <li>
          <m>E_2 = \operatorname{span}\left\{
          \left[\begin{array}{c}1\\0\\-2\end{array}\right],
          \left[\begin{array}{c}0\\1\\2\end{array}\right]
          \right\}</m>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Compute <m>\det(A - \lambda I)</m> to obtain the characteristic polynomial
      <m>\lambda^3 - 7\lambda^2 + 16\lambda - 12.</m>
      Factoring gives roots <m>\lambda = 3</m> and <m>\lambda = 2</m>
      (with multiplicity 2).
    </p>

    <p>
      For <m>\lambda = 3</m>, row reduction of <m>A - 3I</m> yields the eigenspace
      <m>
      E_3 = \operatorname{span}\left\{
      \left[\begin{array}{c}1\\1\\1\end{array}\right]
      \right\}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      For <m>\lambda = 2</m>, the matrix <m>A - 2I</m> reduces to a single equation
      <m>2x_1 - 2x_2 + x_3 = 0</m>, giving the eigenspace
      <m>
      E_2 = \operatorname{span}\left\{
      \left[\begin{array}{c}1\\0\\-2\end{array}\right],
      \left[\begin{array}{c}0\\1\\2\end{array}\right]
      \right\}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus the eigenvalues are <m>3</m> and <m>2</m> (multiplicity 2), with the eigenspaces above.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<!-- 9 -->
  <exercise label="tf-diagonizable-or-not">
    <title>Diagonalizable or Not</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The matrix in the previous problem is diagonalizable. 
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>True.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Yes, the algebraic and geometric mulitiplicities of eigenvalues are the same.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement><p>False.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No.  There are enough eigenvectors to diagonalize.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>

  </exercise>

 <exercise label="system-from-third-order-DE-corrected">
  <title>Transforming and Solving a Third-Order Differential Equation</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the differential equation <m>x''' + 4x' = 3t - 1</m>.  
      Answer the following questions.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        Transform the differential equation into a first-order system
        <m>\mathbf{x}' = A\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{f}(t)</m>.
      </li>

      <li>
        Find a particular solution <m>\mathbf{x}_p(t)</m> to the system.
      </li>

      <li>
        Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of <m>A</m>.
      </li>

      <li>
        Give the general solution to the associated homogeneous system
        <m>\mathbf{x}' = A\mathbf{x}</m>.
      </li>

      <li>
        Use the Wronskian to show that your basis of vector-valued solutions
        is linearly independent.
      </li>

      <li>
        Give a basis for the solution space of the scalar equation
        <m>x''' + 4x' = 0</m> and verify linear independence using the Wronskian.
      </li>

      <li>
        Give the general solution to the system
        <m>\mathbf{x}' = A\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{f}(t)</m>.
        Compare it to the general solution of the original differential equation.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Let <m>x_1 = x</m>, <m>x_2 = x'</m>, and <m>x_3 = x''</m>.  
      Then <m>x_1' = x_2</m>, <m>x_2' = x_3</m>, and 
      <m>x_3' = -4x_2 + 3t - 1</m>.  
      For the homogeneous case, solve <m>r(r^2 + 4)=0</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <m>
          \mathbf{x}' =
          \begin{pmatrix}
            0 \amp 1 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 1\\
            0 \amp -4 \amp 0
          \end{pmatrix}
          \mathbf{x}
          +
          \begin{bmatrix}
            0\\0\\3t - 1
          \end{bmatrix}.
          </m>
        </li>

        <li>
          <m>
          \mathbf{x}_p(t) =
          \begin{bmatrix}
            \frac{3}{8}t^2 - \frac14 t\\
            \frac34 t - \frac14\\
            \frac34
          \end{bmatrix}.
          </m>
        </li>

        <li>
          Eigenvalues:
          <m>\lambda_1 = 0,\ \lambda_2 = 2i,\ \lambda_3 = -2i.</m><br/>
          Eigenvectors:
          <m>
          \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\0\end{bmatrix},\quad
          \begin{bmatrix}-1\\ -2i\\ 4\end{bmatrix},\quad
          \begin{bmatrix}-1\\ 2i\\ 4\end{bmatrix}.
          </m>
        </li>

        <li>
          <m>
          \mathbf{x}_h(t)
          =
          c_1
          \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}
          +
          c_2
          \begin{bmatrix}-\cos(2t)\\ 2\sin(2t)\\ 4\cos(2t)\end{bmatrix}
          +
          c_3
          \begin{bmatrix}-\sin(2t)\\ -2\cos(2t)\\ 4\sin(2t)\end{bmatrix}.
          </m>
        </li>

        <li>
          <m>
          W = 
          \det
          \begin{pmatrix}
            1 \amp -\cos(2t) \amp -\sin(2t)\\
            0 \amp 2\sin(2t) \amp -2\cos(2t)\\
            0 \amp 4\cos(2t) \amp 4\sin(2t)
          \end{pmatrix}
          = 8 \neq 0.
          </m>
        </li>

        <li>
          Basis for <m>x''' + 4x' = 0</m>:
          <m>\{1,\ -\cos(2t),\ -\sin(2t)\}</m>.  
          Wronskian is again <m>8 \neq 0</m>, so independent.
        </li>

        <li>
          <m>
          \mathbf{x}(t) =
          \mathbf{x}_h(t) + \mathbf{x}_p(t).
          </m><br/>
          The first component matches exactly the general solution of
          <m>x''' + 4x' = 3t - 1</m>.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      <strong>(a)</strong><br/>
      Using <m>x_1=x</m>, <m>x_2=x'</m>, <m>x_3=x''</m> gives
      <m>
      \mathbf{x}' =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        0 \amp 1 \amp 0\\
        0 \amp 0 \amp 1\\
        0 \amp -4 \amp 0
      \end{pmatrix}
      \mathbf{x}
      +
      \begin{bmatrix}
        0\\0\\3t-1
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(b)</strong><br/>
      Using the known particular solution of the scalar ODE gives
      <m>
      x_p(t) = \frac{3}{8}t^2 - \frac14 t,
      </m>
      and its derivatives lead to
      <m>
      \mathbf{x}_p(t) =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        \frac{3}{8}t^2 - \frac14 t\\
        \frac34 t - \frac14\\
        \frac34
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(c)</strong><br/>
      The characteristic equation is
      <m>- \lambda^3 - 4\lambda = -\lambda(\lambda^2 + 4)</m>,
      giving eigenvalues <m>0, 2i, -2i</m>.  
      The eigenvectors listed in the answer are verified directly.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(d)</strong><br/>
      Converting complex solutions to real form yields the homogeneous solution:
      <m>
      \mathbf{x}_h(t)
      =
      c_1\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}
      +
      c_2\begin{bmatrix}-\cos(2t)\\2\sin(2t)\\4\cos(2t)\end{bmatrix}
      +
      c_3\begin{bmatrix}-\sin(2t)\\-2\cos(2t)\\4\sin(2t)\end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(e)</strong><br/>
      Computing the Wronskian gives
      <m>
      W = 8 \neq 0,
      </m>
      confirming linear independence.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(f)</strong><br/>
      For the scalar DE <m>x''' + 4x' = 0</m>, the solution basis is
      <m>\{1,\ -\cos(2t),\ -\sin(2t)\}</m>,  
      with the same Wronskian <m>8</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>(g)</strong><br/>
      The general solution to the system is
      <m>\mathbf{x}(t) = \mathbf{x}_h(t) + \mathbf{x}_p(t)</m>.
      Its first component matches the general solution of the original
      differential equation.
    </p>

  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="laplace-from-graph">
  <title>Computing a Laplace Transform from a Graph</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Compute the Laplace transform of the function <m>f(t)</m> graphed in <xref ref="GraphLaplaceTransformF"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="GraphLaplaceTransformF" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
      <caption>The graph of <m>f(t)</m>.</caption>
      <image width="50%">
        <description>A line segment from (0,0) to (1,1) and 0 for t greater than 1.</description>
        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[thick,scale=1.2] 
   \draw [black!60,line width=0.3pt,-latex] (-1,0) -- (4,0) node [below] at (3.75,0) {$t$};
   \draw [black!60,line width=0.3pt,-latex] (0,-1) -- (0,3) node [left] at (0,2.75) {$f(t)$};
   \draw [linecolor,line width=2pt] (0,0) -- (1,1); 
   \draw [linecolor,line width=2pt,-latex] (1.1,0) -- (4,0); 
   \fill [linecolor] (0,0) circle (3pt);
   \fill [linecolor] (1,1) circle (3pt) node [right] at (1,1) {$(1,1)$};
\draw [linecolor] (1,0) circle (3pt);
\foreach \x/\xtext in {1/1, 2/2, 3/3}
    \draw[shift={(\x,0)}] (0pt,2pt) -- (0pt,-2pt) node[below] {$\xtext$};
\foreach \y/\ytext in {1/1, 2/2}
    \draw[shift={(0,\y)}] (-2pt,0pt) -- (2pt,0pt) node[left] {$\ytext$};
\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
</figure>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Express <m>f(t)</m> as a piecewise function.  
      Only the interval <m>0 \le t \le 1</m> contributes to the Laplace transform.  
      Compute <m>\int_0^1 t e^{-st}\,dt</m> using integration by parts.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
      \mathcal{L}\{f(t)\}
      =
      \frac{1}{s^2}
      -
      \left(\frac{1}{s}+\frac{1}{s^2}\right)e^{-s}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      From the graph,
      <me>
      f(t)=
      \begin{cases}
        t, \amp 0 \le t \le 1,\\
        0, \amp t &gt; 1.
      \end{cases}
      </me>
      Thus,
      <m>
      \mathcal{L}\{f(t)\}
      = \int_0^1 t e^{-st}\,dt.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Using integration by parts with
      <m>u=t</m>, <m>dv=e^{-st}dt</m>,
      we obtain
      <m>
      \int t e^{-st}\,dt
      = -\frac{t}{s}e^{-st}
        - \frac{1}{s^2}e^{-st}.
      </m>
      Evaluating from <m>0</m> to <m>1</m> gives
      <m>
      \int_0^1 t e^{-st}dt
      = -\left(\frac{1}{s}+\frac{1}{s^2}\right)e^{-s}
        + \frac{1}{s^2}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore,
      <m>
      \mathcal{L}\{f(t)\}
      =
      \frac{1}{s^2}
      -
      \left(\frac{1}{s}+\frac{1}{s^2}\right)e^{-s}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="laplace-IVP-second-order">
  <title>Solving an IVP Using Laplace Transforms</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Solve the IVP
      <md>
        <mrow>x'' - x' - 6x = 0,</mrow> 
        <mrow>x(0) = 2, x'(0) = -1,</mrow>
      </md>
      using Laplace transforms.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Take the Laplace transform of both sides.  Recall the formulas
      <m>\mathcal{L}\{x'\} = sX(s) - x(0)</m> and 
      <m>\mathcal{L}\{x''\} = s^2 X(s) - s x(0) - x'(0)</m>.
      Solve for <m>X(s)</m> and invert.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
      x(t) = \frac{7}{5} e^{3t} + \frac{3}{5} e^{-2t}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Take the Laplace transform of the IVP:
      <m>
      \mathcal{L}\{x''\} - \mathcal{L}\{x'\} - 6\mathcal{L}\{x\} = 0.
      </m>
      Using
      <m>
      \mathcal{L}\{x''\} = s^2 X(s) - s x(0) - x'(0),
      \quad
      \mathcal{L}\{x'\} = sX(s) - x(0),
      </m>
      we obtain
      <m>
      (s^2 X - 2s + 1) - (sX - 2) - 6X = 0.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Simplify:
      <m>
      s^2 X - sX - 6X - 2s + 3 = 0
      \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
      X(s)(s^2 - s - 6) = 2s - 3.
      </m>
      Since
      <m>s^2 - s - 6 = (s - 3)(s + 2),</m>
      we have
      <m>
      X(s) = \frac{2s - 3}{(s - 3)(s + 2)}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Use partial fractions.  Solve for constants <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> such that
      <m>
      \frac{2s - 3}{(s - 3)(s + 2)}
      =
      \frac{A}{s - 3} + \frac{B}{s + 2}.
      </m>
      Then
      <m>
      2s - 3 = A(s + 2) + B(s - 3).
      </m>
      Matching coefficients gives
      <m>A = \frac{7}{5}, B = \frac{3}{5}.</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus
      <m>
      X(s) = \frac{7/5}{s - 3} + \frac{3/5}{s + 2}.
      </m>
      Inverting the Laplace transform yields
      <m>
      x(t) = \frac{7}{5} e^{3t} + \frac{3}{5}e^{-2t}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, the solution of the IVP is
      <m>
      x(t) = \frac{7}{5} e^{3t} + \frac{3}{5} e^{-2t}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="inverse-laplace-2s+1-over-s-s2+9">
  <title>Inverse Laplace Transform</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the inverse Laplace transform of
      <me>
        F(s) = \frac{2s+1}{s(s^2+9)}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Use partial fractions:
      <me>
      \frac{2s+1}{s(s^2+9)}
      = \frac{A}{s} + \frac{Bs + C}{s^2+9}.
      </me>
      Then invert each term using basic Laplace transform formulas.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
      f(t) = \frac{1}{9} - \frac{1}{9}\cos(3t) + \frac{2}{3}\sin(3t).
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Begin with partial fractions:
      <me>
      \frac{2s+1}{s(s^2+9)}
      = \frac{A}{s} + \frac{Bs + C}{s^2+9}.
      </me>
      Solving for coefficients yields
      <m>
      A = \frac{1}{9},
      B = -\frac{1}{9},
      C = 2.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus
      <me>
      \frac{2s+1}{s(s^2+9)}
      =
      \frac{1}{9}\frac{1}{s}
      - \frac{1}{9}\frac{s}{s^2+9}
      + 2\frac{1}{s^2+9}.
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      Taking inverse Laplace transforms term‑by‑term:
      <me>
      \mathcal{L}^{-1}\left\{\frac{1}{s}\right\} = 1,
      \mathcal{L}^{-1}\left\{\frac{s}{s^2+9}\right\} = \cos(3t),
      \mathcal{L}^{-1}\left\{\frac{1}{s^2+9}\right\} = \frac{1}{3}\sin(3t).
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore,
      <me>
      f(t)
      = \frac{1}{9}
        - \frac{1}{9}\cos(3t)
        + \frac{2}{3}\sin(3t).
      </me>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="inverse-laplace-shifted-1-over-s-squared">
  <title>Inverse Laplace Transform with a Shift</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the inverse Laplace transform <m>f(t)</m> of
      <m>
        F(s) = \frac{e^{-3s}}{s^2}.
      </m>
      Then sketch the graph of <m>f(t)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Use the time–shift theorem:
      <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\{e^{-as}F(s)\} = u(t-a)f(t-a).</m>
      First compute <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\{1/s^2\}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
      f(t) = u(t-3)\,\cdot (t-3).
      </m>
      The graph is zero for <m>t &lt; 3</m>, and is the line <m>t-3</m> for <m>t \ge 3</m> as in <xref ref="StepTimeTm3"/>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Since
      <m>
        \mathcal{L}^{-1}\left\{\frac{1}{s^2}\right\} = t,
      </m>
      we apply the time–shift rule:
      <m>
        \mathcal{L}^{-1}\{e^{-3s}F(s)\}
        = u(t-3)\,f(t-3).
      </m>
      Here <m>f(t)=t</m>, so
      <m>
        f(t)
        = u(t-3)\,\cdot (t-3).
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      The graph is:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><m>f(t)=0</m> for <m>t &lt; 3</m>,</li>
      <li><m>f(t)=t-3</m> for <m>t\ge 3</m>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The graph is a ray starting at the point <m>(3,0)</m> and rising with
      slope <m>1</m> as shown in <xref ref="StepTimeTm3"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="StepTimeTm3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
      <caption>The graph of <m>f(t) = u(t-3)\cdot(t-3)</m>.</caption>
      <image width="50%">
        <description>A graph is shown with the horizontal axis labeled t and the vertical axis labeled f of t. From t equals negative one up to t equals three, the graph lies on the horizontal axis, meaning f of t equals zero. At the point t equals three, f of t is still zero, but from that point the graph begins rising in a straight line with slope one. The rising line starts at the point three comma zero and goes upward to the point four comma one. Tick marks appear at t equals one, two, and three on the horizontal axis, and at f equals one and f equals two on the vertical axis.</description>
<latex-image>
  \begin{tikzpicture}[thick,scale=1.0] 
   \draw [black!60,line width=0.3pt,-latex] (-1,0) -- (4,0) node [below] at (3.75,0) {$t$};
   \draw [black!60,line width=0.3pt,-latex] (0,-1) -- (0,3) node [left] at (0,2.75) {$f(t)$};
   \draw [linecolor,line width=2pt] (-1,0) -- (3,0); 
   \draw [linecolor,line width=2pt,-latex] (3,0) -- (4,1); 
\foreach \x/\xtext in {1/1, 2/2, 3/3}
    \draw[shift={(\x,0)}] (0pt,2pt) -- (0pt,-2pt) node[below] {$\xtext$};
\foreach \y/\ytext in {1/1, 2/2}
    \draw[shift={(0,\y)}] (-2pt,0pt) -- (2pt,0pt) node[left] {$\ytext$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="slope-field-yprime-t-plus-y">
  <title>Solution Curve and Existence–Uniqueness</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      The slope field for the differential equation <m>y' = t + y</m> is shown
      in <xref ref="SlopeFieldtPy"/>. Sketch the likely solution curve to the IVP
      <md><mrow>y' = t + y,</mrow><mrow>y(0) = -\tfrac12</mrow></md> on the slope field.
      Then determine whether a unique solution to this IVP exists.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="SlopeFieldtPy">
      <caption>The slope field for <m>y'=t+y</m>.</caption>
<image width="60%">
  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="prefigure-rectangle">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="12">
  <definition>f(t,y) = t+y</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
    <grid-axes/>
    <slope-field function="f"/>
  </coordinates>
  <annotations>
    <annotation ref="figure"
                text="A slope field in the t-y plane">
      <annotation ref="graph-group" text="The short line segments grow steeper
          and tilt upward as both t and y increase, consistent with the equation
          y' = t + y">
        <annotation ref="graph" text="Near t = 0 and y = -1/2 the slopes are slightly negative,
          becoming positive as t increases"/>
      </annotation>
    </annotation>
  </annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Rewrite the DE as <m>y' - y = t</m> and recall that the existence and
      uniqueness theorem applies because the right-hand side 
      <m>f(t,y) = t + y</m> is continuous and satisfies
      <m>\partial f / \partial y = 1</m>, which is also continuous everywhere.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      A unique solution exists for all real <m>t</m>, and its graph is an
      increasing curve passing through the point <m>(0,-\tfrac12)</m>.  
      The explicit solution is
      <me>y(t) = t - 1 + \tfrac12 e^{t}.</me>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      To sketch the solution curve on the slope field, locate the initial
      condition <m>(0,-\tfrac12)</m>.  
      The slope field shows slightly negative slopes there, but slopes increase
      rapidly as <m>t</m> increases.  
      Following the slope field, the curve dips very slightly and then rises
      smoothly upward as it moves right.
    </p>

    <p>
      Solve the IVP explicitly.  Rewrite the differential equation as
      <m>y' - y = t</m>.  
      The integrating factor is <m>e^{-t}</m>.  
      Multiplying both sides by <m>e^{-t}</m> gives
      <m>(e^{-t}y)' = t e^{-t}.</m>
      Integrating:
      <m>
        e^{-t}y = -t e^{-t} - e^{-t} + C.
      </m>
      Multiply by <m>e^{t}</m>:
      <m>
        y = -t - 1 + C e^{t}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Apply the initial condition <m>y(0) = -\tfrac12</m>:
      <m>
        -1 + C = -\tfrac12 \Rightarrow C = \tfrac12.
      </m>
      Therefore the unique solution is:
      <m>
        y(t) = t - 1 + \tfrac12 e^{t}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Because <m>f(t,y)=t+y</m> and <m>\partial f/\partial y = 1</m> are both
      continuous on all of <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>, the Existence–Uniqueness Theorem
      guarantees that a unique solution exists through every point, including
      <m>(0,-\tfrac12)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus the IVP has a unique solution, and the solution curve on the slope
      field is the one that passes through <m>(0,-\tfrac12)</m> and follows the
      increasing directions of the field.
    </p>

     <figure xml:id="SlopeFieldtPySolution">
      <caption>The slope field for <m>y'=t+y</m> with a particular solution to an IVP.</caption>
<image width="60%">
  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="prefigure-rectangle2">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="12">
  <definition>f(t,y) = t+y</definition>
   <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
    <grid-axes/>
    <slope-field function="f"/>
    <de-solve function="f" t0="0" y0="-0.5" t1="4" name="linear"/>
    <plot-de-solution solution="linear" stroke="red"/>
    <plot-de-solution function="f" t0="0" y0="-0.5" t1="4" stroke="red"/>
  </coordinates>
  <annotations>
    <annotation ref="figure"
                text="A slope field in the t-y plane">
      <annotation ref="graph-group" text=" The short line segments grow steeper
          and tilt upward as both t and y increase, consistent with the equation
          y' = t + y">
        <annotation ref="graph" text="Near t = 0 and y = -1/2 the slopes are slightly negative,
          becoming positive as t increases"/>
        <annotation ref="graph" text="Near t = 0 and y = -1/2 the solution first curves down and then curves up"/>
      </annotation>
    </annotation>
  </annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="autonomous-equilibria-stability">
  <title>Autonomous Differential Equation and Equilibria</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the differential equation
      <me>
        \dfrac{dx}{dt} = k(N - x)(x - H),
      </me>
      where <m>k &gt; 0</m> and <m>H &lt; N</m> are constants.
      Determine whether this DE is autonomous.  
      If it is, find all equilibrium solutions and classify each as stable or unstable.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      A differential equation is autonomous if the right-hand side depends only on
      <m>x</m> and constants, not on <m>t</m>.
      Equilibria occur where <m>dx/dt = 0</m>.
      Use a sign chart on <m>k(N-x)(x-H)</m> to determine stability.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The DE is autonomous.  
      The equilibrium solutions are <m>x = H</m> and <m>x = N</m>.  
      <m>x = H</m> is unstable, and <m>x = N</m> is stable.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The right-hand side of the DE,
      <m>f(x) = k(N - x)(x - H),</m>
      depends only on <m>x</m> (and constants), not on <m>t</m>, so the equation is
      autonomous.
    </p>

    <p>
      Equilibria occur where <m>dx/dt = 0</m>.  Thus,
      <m>
        k(N - x)(x - H) = 0
      </m>
      implies
      <m>x = H</m> or <m>x = N</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      To classify the equilibria, examine the sign of <m>f(x)</m> on the intervals
      determined by <m>H</m> and <m>N</m>.  
      Because <m>k &gt; 0</m>, the sign of <m>dx/dt</m> is the sign of
      <m>(N - x)(x - H)</m>.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        For <m>x &lt; H</m>,
        <m>N - x &gt; 0</m> and <m>x - H &lt; 0</m>, so <m>dx/dt &lt; 0</m>.  
        Solutions move left, away from <m>x = H</m>.
      </li>
      <li>
        For <m>H &lt; x &lt; N</m>,
        both <m>N - x</m> and <m>x - H</m> are positive, so <m>dx/dt &gt; 0</m>.  
        Solutions move right, again away from <m>x = H</m>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Thus <m>x = H</m> is <em>unstable</em>.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        For <m>x &gt; N</m>,
        <m>N - x &lt; 0</m> and <m>x - H &gt; 0</m>, so <m>dx/dt &lt; 0</m>.  
        Solutions move left, toward <m>x = N</m>.
      </li>
      <li>
        For <m>H &lt; x &lt; N</m>,
        <m>dx/dt &gt; 0</m>, so solutions move right, again toward <m>x = N</m>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Thus <m>x = N</m> is <em>stable</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, the equation is autonomous, with equilibrium solutions
      <m>x = H</m> (unstable) and <m>x = N</m> (stable).
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="ivp-integrating-factor-yprime-plus-2x-y-equals-x">
  <title>Solving an IVP with an Integrating Factor</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Solve the IVP
      <me>y' + 2x y = x,\quad y(0) = -2.</me>
      <em>Hint:</em> Use an integrating factor.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Write the DE in the form <m>y' + p(x)y = q(x)</m>.  
      The integrating factor is <m>\mu(x) = e^{\int p(x)\,dx} = e^{x^2}</m>.  
      Multiply the entire equation by <m>e^{x^2}</m> and look for an exact derivative.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
      y(x) = -2 e^{-x^2} + \frac{1}{2} e^{-x^2} \int_{0}^{x} t\, e^{t^2}\, dt.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The differential equation is already in linear form:
      <m>
        y' + 2x y = x.
      </m>
      Here <m>p(x) = 2x</m>, so the integrating factor is
      <m>
        \mu(x) = e^{\int 2x\,dx} = e^{x^2}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Multiply the entire DE by <m>e^{x^2}</m>:
      <m>
        e^{x^2} y' + 2x e^{x^2} y = x e^{x^2}.
      </m>
      The left-hand side is the derivative of <m>e^{x^2} y</m>:
      <m>
        \frac{d}{dx}\!\left( e^{x^2} y \right)
        = x e^{x^2}.
      </m>
      Integrate both sides:
      <m>
        e^{x^2} y 
        = \int x e^{x^2}\,dx + C.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Compute the integral:
      <m>
        \int x e^{x^2}\,dx = \frac12 e^{x^2}.
      </m>
      Thus
      <m>
        e^{x^2} y = \frac12 e^{x^2} + C.
      </m>
      Solve for <m>y</m>:
      <m>
        y = \frac12 + C e^{-x^2}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Apply <m>y(0) = -2</m>:
      <m>
        -2 = \frac12 + C \quad\Rightarrow\quad C = -\frac52.
      </m>
      Therefore,
      <m>
        y(x) = \frac12 - \frac52 e^{-x^2}.
      </m>
      This can also be written in integral form as
      <m>
        y(x)
        = -2 e^{-x^2}
        + \frac12 e^{-x^2}
          \int_0^x t e^{t^2}\,dt.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Hence, the solution to the IVP is:
      <m>
        y(x) = \frac12 - \frac52 e^{-x^2}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="real-valued-solution-operator-factorization">
  <title>Real-Valued General Solution from Operator Factorization</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find a real-valued general solution to
      <m>
        (D - 1)^3 (D^2 - 4)(D^2 - 4D + 5)\,y = 0.
      </m>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Each factor of the operator corresponds to a characteristic root.
      Use multiplicity to determine how many linearly independent solutions come
      from each root.  
      For quadratic factors, solve the corresponding characteristic equation.
      Convert complex solutions into real-valued sine and cosine forms.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      A real-valued general solution is
      <m>
      y(t) =
      C_1 e^{t} + C_2 t e^{t} + C_3 t^2 e^{t}
      + C_4 e^{2t} + C_5 e^{-2t}
      + e^{2t}\!\left(C_6 \cos t + C_7 \sin t\right).
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The operator factors give characteristic roots directly.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <m>(D - 1)^3</m> gives the repeated root <m>r = 1</m> of multiplicity 3.
        Thus the corresponding solutions are
        <m>
          e^{t},\; t e^{t},\; t^{2} e^{t}.
        </m>
      </li>

      <li>
        <m>(D^{2} - 4)</m> gives real roots <m>r = 2</m> and <m>r = -2</m>,
        yielding solutions
        <m>e^{2t}</m> and <m>e^{-2t}</m>.
      </li>

      <li>
        <m>(D^{2} - 4D + 5)</m> gives roots
        <m>
        r = \frac{4 \pm \sqrt{16 - 20}}{2}
          = 2 \pm i.
        </m>
        These produce the complex solutions <m>e^{(2 \pm i)t}</m>, which combine to
        the real-valued pair
        <m>e^{2t}\cos t</m> and <m>e^{2t}\sin t</m>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Combining all real solutions gives the complete real-valued general solution:
      <m>
      y(t)
      =
      C_1 e^{t}
      + C_2 t e^{t}
      + C_3 t^{2} e^{t}
      + C_4 e^{2t}
      + C_5 e^{-2t}
      + e^{2t}\!\left(C_6 \cos t + C_7 \sin t\right).
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Since these solutions arise from distinct roots (or multiplicities of a root),
      and each corresponds to a linearly independent solution of the
      linear homogeneous ODE, they form a basis for the solution space.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise label="eigenvalue-eigenvector-system-2x2">
  <title>General Solution via Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Use the eigenvalue–eigenvector method to find a general solution to the system
      </p>
      <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      x_1' \amp= x_1 + 2x_2,\\
      x_2' \amp= 2x_1 + x_2.
      \end{aligned}
      </me>
      <p>
      Is the equilibrium solution <m>x_1(t)\equiv 0</m>, <m>x_2(t)\equiv 0</m> stable or not?
      </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Write the system in matrix form <m>\mathbf{x}' = A\mathbf{x}</m> and compute the
      eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.  
      Distinct real eigenvalues give independent eigenvectors, and the sign of each eigenvalue
      determines stability of the origin.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The eigenvalues are <m>\lambda_1 = 3</m> and <m>\lambda_2 = -1</m>, with eigenvectors
      <m>\left[\begin{array}{c}1\\1\end{array}\right]</m> and
      <m>\left[\begin{array}{c}1\\-1\end{array}\right]</m>.
      The general solution is
      <m>
      \mathbf{x}(t)
      = C_1 e^{3t}\!\left[\begin{array}{c}1\\1\end{array}\right]
      + C_2 e^{-t}\!\left[\begin{array}{c}1\\-1\end{array}\right].
      </m>
      The equilibrium at the origin is a saddle point and therefore unstable.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Write the system in matrix form:
      <me>
      \mathbf{x}' = A\mathbf{x},\qquad
      A =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        1 \amp 2\\
        2 \amp 1
      \end{pmatrix}.
      </me>
      Compute the characteristic polynomial:
      <me>
      \det(A - \lambda I)
      = \det\begin{pmatrix}1-\lambda \amp 2\\2 \amp 1-\lambda\end{pmatrix}
      = (1-\lambda)^2 - 4
      = \lambda^2 - 2\lambda - 3.
      </me>
      Factor:
      <m>
      \lambda^2 - 2\lambda - 3 = (\lambda - 3)(\lambda + 1).
      </m>
      Thus the eigenvalues are <m>\lambda_1 = 3</m> and <m>\lambda_2 = -1</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For <m>\lambda_1 = 3</m>, solve <m>(A - 3I)\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}</m>:
      <m>
      A - 3I =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        -2 \amp 2\\
        2 \amp -2
      \end{pmatrix},
      </m>
      which yields eigenvector
      <m>\left[\begin{array}{c}1\\1\end{array}\right].</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      For <m>\lambda_2 = -1</m>, solve <m>(A + I)\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}</m>:
      <m>
      A + I =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        2 \amp 2\\
        2 \amp 2
      \end{pmatrix},
      </m>
      giving eigenvector
      <m>\left[\begin{array}{c}1\\-1\end{array}\right].</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, the general solution is
      <m>
      \mathbf{x}(t)
      = C_1 e^{3t}\!\left[\begin{array}{c}1\\1\end{array}\right]
      + C_2 e^{-t}\!\left[\begin{array}{c}1\\-1\end{array}\right].
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      Since one eigenvalue is positive (<m>3</m>) and one is negative (<m>-1</m>),
      solutions grow exponentially in the direction of the eigenvector for <m>\lambda = 3</m>
      and decay in the direction of the eigenvector for <m>\lambda = -1</m>.
      This makes the origin a saddle point.
    </p>

    <p>
      A saddle point is always unstable.  
      Thus the equilibrium solution <m>\mathbf{x}(t)=\mathbf{0}</m> is unstable.
    </p>

  </solution>

</exercise>

  </exercises>

